+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt on each kiosk from the active snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory — one centrally maintained index and smaller kiosk updates, but lookups depend on the lab network and need service-timeout handling; Managed search service — less index maintenance and room for richer search later, but recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity become registry concerns | Only the embedded index keeps every lookup satisfying the HLD's no-runtime-network-access tenet; the other two make a lookup depend on the lab network or an external service during normal kiosk operation. Within that constraint, predictable lookups and no service to run outweigh each kiosk having to validate and rebuild its own index on snapshot arrival. The certificate interface and result type are unaffected by this choice. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. What happens to the in-progress new index file if a kiosk loses power mid-rebuild during import — is a partial file reliably distinguished from a valid one on next startup?
+2. Does lookup detect a corrupted active index (e.g., disk fault between imports) and report an error, or fail silently?
+3. Is there enough disk headroom to hold the new index alongside the still-active one during a rebuild, given snapshots up to 150 MB?
+4. Does the SQLite index carry its own format/version tag, independent of the snapshot schema version, in case a future software update changes the index format without a new snapshot?
